"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.01993893492733729,"_gp_sbc_dilution":true,"_score_sbc_dilution":66.8,"_gate_insider_own":0.00934,"_gp_insider_own":false,"_score_insider_own":6.2,"_gate_insider_buying":null,"_gp_insider_buying":null,"_score_insider_buying":null,"pf":[],"description_full":"Global Payments Inc. provides payment technology and software solutions for card, check, and digital-based payments in the Americas, Europe, and the Asia-Pacific. It offers authorization, settlement and funding, customer support, chargeback resolution, reconciliation and dispute management, terminal rental, sales and deployment, payment security, and consolidated billing and reporting services. The company also provides an array of enterprise software solutions that streamline business operations of its customers in various vertical markets; and value-added solutions and services, such as point-of-sale software, analytics and customer engagement, payroll and reporting, and human capital management. It markets its products and services through direct sales force, trade associations, agent and enterprise software providers, referral arrangements with value-added resellers, independent sales organizations, payment facilitators, and financial institutions. The company was founded in 1967 and is headquartered in Atlanta, Georgia.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Cameron M. Bready CPA | CEO & Director | Age 53 | Compensation: $4.2M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 26K people. Revenue per employee of $296K ranks in the below the sector median among Industrials peers, with revenue growing 11%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $78K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $6K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 14\u00d7 revenue per employee (reasonable relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (10/10).","financial_summary":["Global Payments Inc. is a large-cap industrials business with a $24.3B market cap on $7.7B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at -5% over the past three years and +11% annually across the full decade, and a shrinking top line is the hardest headwind any business can overcome.","Returns on capital run at 4% against a 7% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests.","The business throws off $2.0B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 26 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.90\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, debt is moderate at 3.8\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, serviceable in normal conditions but worth watching through a downturn, and thin 2.8\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Management returns a meaningful 5.9% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, a strong signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome, and within its sector the business captures 2.0\u00d7 its fair share of profits, a structural advantage visible in the numbers."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (1.0) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Payout ratio looks stretched \u2014 the dividend\u2019s own margin of safety is thin","sev":"red","cat":"dividend"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 6.5% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Revenue growth lags most Industrials peers \u2014 losing share inside the profit pool","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Industrials sector lagging the market significantly, underperforming by 6% over 3 months","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Cyclicality and capital intensity \u2014 order books turn on a dime, and the heavy fixed cost base that creates the moat also magnifies the downturn.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Supply chain reshoring costs and labour shortages \u2014 onshoring mandates raise capex requirements while a shrinking skilled-labour pool inflates operating costs.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Tariff and trade-policy exposure \u2014 industrial exporters sit squarely in the crosshairs of protectionist policy, and retaliatory tariffs can close markets without warning.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Operating margins 14% above sector median \u2014 the quantitative trace of pricing power","Trading at a 46% margin of safety to estimated fair value \u2014 a real cushion against errors in the estimate","Market is pricing in lower growth than the company has historically delivered","Valued at a 35% discount to sector median EV/EBITDA","24 consecutive years of dividend payments \u2014 an operating record that makes the dividend claim credible"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Should Global Payments (GPN) Stock Be Reassessed After AI Moves And Elliott Pressure?","source":"simplywall.st","link":"https://simplywall.st/stocks/us/diversified-financials/nyse-gpn/global-payments/news/should-global-payments-gpn-stock-be-reassessed-after-ai-move","date":"2026-11-13","timestamp":1794583303.814,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.802,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Global Payments (GPN) Sheds Double Digits. These 2 Firms May Hold the Reason","source":"insidermonkey.com","link":"https://www.insidermonkey.com/news/global-payments-gpn-sheds-double-digits-these-2-firms-may-hold-the-reason-1865144/","date":"2026-11-13","timestamp":1794531090.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Is Global Payments (NYSE:GPN) Entering A New Growth Phase?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/us/stocks/industrial/is